# Line endings, pinned by the REPOSITORY rather than by each machine's git
# config.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Every text blob committed here is LF, but the worktree
# on a Windows checkout is CRLF, because core.autocrlf=true is set in this
# machine's global and system git config. Those two facts are only compatible
# while the reading git actually applies that config: it converts CRLF back to
# LF when comparing, so the file reads as unchanged.
#
# A git that does NOT see that config — WSL reading /mnt, a GUI client with a
# bundled git, a CI container, `git -c core.autocrlf=false` — does no such
# conversion, so it sees a CRLF worktree against an LF index and reports EVERY
# LINE of the file as deleted and re-added. That produced a 273-file,
# ~70,900-line phantom diff on a branch whose real change was 8 files, and it
# hid the actual review behind pure noise. (It also only appears for files whose
# stat cache has gone stale, which is why the count moves around and why it can
# look like something just rewrote the tree. Nothing had.)
#
# `text=auto` fixes it because it forces checkin normalisation REGARDLESS of
# core.autocrlf: every git now converts to LF on the way in, so every git agrees
# the file is unchanged. Deliberately no global `eol=` — that would additionally
# pin the working copy and rewrite ~266 files on disk for no benefit, since the
# index is already LF and the diff is what needed fixing.
* text=auto

# Windows batch files are the one real exception: cmd.exe can mis-parse a .bat
# with LF endings (a label or a multi-line block can be read wrong), so these
# must land CRLF in the working copy. Stored LF in the repo like everything
# else — matching what is already committed for scripts/update.bat and
# start-stop.bat.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts are the mirror image: a CRLF shebang line breaks execution on
# Linux, and these run inside the containers.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Never touch these. They are already detected as binary today (the five files
# under apps/seo-desk), but detection is a heuristic and a corrupted font or
# image is not obvious in review — so it is stated rather than inferred.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.eot binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.xlsx binary
